.TH NP_FILTER 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_filter_bpf, np_filter_proto, np_filter_port, np_filter_host, np_filter_and, np_filter_or, np_filter_not, np_filter_free, np_pipeline_add_filter \- netpipe packet filtering
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_bpf(const char *" expr ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_proto(np_proto_t " proto ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_port(uint16_t " port ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_host(const char *" host ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_and(np_filter_t *" a ", np_filter_t *" b ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_or(np_filter_t *" a ", np_filter_t *" b ");"
.br
.BI "np_filter_t *np_filter_not(np_filter_t *" a ");"
.br
.BI "void np_filter_free(np_filter_t *" f ");"
.br
.BI "np_err_t np_pipeline_add_filter(np_pipeline_t *" pl ", np_filter_t *" f ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions create, compose, and attach packet filters to a pipeline.
Filters discard packets that do not match specific criteria, preventing them from being processed by subsequent processors and sinks.
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_bpf ()
function compiles a standard Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) expression specified by
.IR expr .
The syntax matches tcpdump or libpcap capture filter syntax (e.g., "tcp port 443 and host 8.8.8.8").
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_proto ()
function creates a high-level protocol filter that matches packets containing the protocol specified by
.I proto
somewhere in the layer stack (e.g.,
.BR NP_PROTO_DNS ).
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_port ()
function creates a filter that matches packets whose TCP or UDP source or destination port equals
.IR port .
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_host ()
function creates a filter that matches packets whose source or destination IPv4 address matches the string specified by
.IR host .
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_and (),
.BR np_filter_or (),
and
.BR np_filter_not ()
functions allow logical composition of filters.
They combine or invert existing filters to build arbitrary filter trees.
The input filter pointers are consumed by these calls (i.e. they become owned by the returned composite filter, and the caller must not free them).
.PP
The
.BR np_filter_free ()
function releases the resources associated with the specified filter.
Filters added to a pipeline via
.BR np_pipeline_add_filter ()
are automatically managed by the pipeline and will be freed when
.BR np_pipeline_free ()
is called; the caller must not free them.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_add_filter ()
function adds a filter to the pipeline.
Multiple filters added to a pipeline are logically ANDed together sequentially.
.SH RETURN VALUES
The filter creation functions return a pointer to a newly allocated
.B np_filter_t
on success, or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
.BR np_pipeline_add_filter ()
returns
.B NP_OK
on success, or a negative
.B np_err_t
code on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_pipeline (3),
.BR np_packet (3)
